WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.104
Begin with the inspection question.

00:00:03.104 --> 00:00:09.072
Define the asset, component, suspected condition, decision, timeframe, and intended reviewer.

00:00:09.072 --> 00:00:18.860
A request to inspect a bridge is too broad; a useful objective identifies which areas and evidence are needed.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:24.352
Required detail determines sensor, lens, distance, angle, speed, and coverage.

00:00:24.352 --> 00:00:35.095
A broad condition survey needs context, while a small component may need close visual evidence collected from a safe authorized standoff.

00:00:35.095 --> 00:00:38.198
Review access and safety constraints.

00:00:38.198 --> 00:00:47.509
Consider controlled areas, traffic, rail, electrical equipment, confined spaces, emissions, moving machinery, people, wildlife, terrain, and emergency operations.

00:00:47.509 --> 00:00:53.000
The drone does not remove ground risk or site coordination.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:01.834
Define deliverables before flight: overview images, close-ups, video, thermal files, maps, annotations, defect tables, or comparison products.

00:01:01.834 --> 00:01:07.325
Specify resolution, location context, naming, metadata, confidence, and specialist-review needs.

00:01:07.325 --> 00:01:13.293
Stakeholders should agree on what the drone can and cannot establish.

00:01:13.293 --> 00:01:18.784
A visual survey may prioritize follow-up without certifying asset condition.

00:01:18.784 --> 00:01:24.275
Thermal screening may identify an anomaly without diagnosing its cause.

00:01:24.275 --> 00:01:31.676
Set explicit exclusions for private areas, unrelated infrastructure, sensitive security features, and unnecessary people.

00:01:31.676 --> 00:01:37.167
Scope controls flight time, privacy, data volume, and analytical noise.

00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:42.181
Changes should be approved rather than improvised during collection.

00:01:42.181 --> 00:01:45.762
Define acceptance criteria for the deliverable.

00:01:45.762 --> 00:01:58.415
Required components, maximum missing coverage, target pixel detail, reference views, annotation rules, turnaround, and review authority should be understood before the crew leaves the site.
